  <section class="site-section about-heritage-work">
    <div class="canadiana-shell">
      <div class="about-heritage-work__grid">
        <div class="about-heritage-work__media">
          <a href="https://n2t.net/ark:/69429/m0zp3vt1hc62" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            <img
              src="<%= asset_path('about/heritage/oocihm.lac_reel_c13525.63.png') %>"
              alt="Handwritten archival letter from the Héritage Collection"
              loading="lazy"
            >
          </a>
        </div>

        <article class="about-heritage-work__copy" aria-labelledby="heritage-work-title">
          <h2 id="heritage-work-title">How to Use Héritage</h2>
          <ol>
            <li>
              To get started, enter your search terms into the Héritage search box. The basic search helps guide you to
              collections that match your keywords. It searches descriptive information, microfilm reel numbers,
              metadata, and, where available, document text. To refine your query, use techniques from
              <a href="<%= navigating_collections_path(lang: I18n.locale) %>">Navigating the Collections</a>, including
              exact phrases in quotes and operators like <code>?</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>-</code>, and
              <code>|</code>. You can also explore Héritage by subject matter by browsing the five thematic areas above
              (Genealogy Materials, Indigenous Peoples, Government Departments, Military History, and Prominent Canadians).
            </li>
            <li>
              Next, choose the archival collection that best matches your research interest. Each individual collection page includes information about the collection as well as archival references. This information, drawn
              from <a href="https://recherche-collection-search.bac-lac.gc.ca/eng/home/index" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Library and Archives Canada's Collection Search catalogue</a>, was generously shared to help ease of access. If you have started your search on LAC's Collection Search, please use identifiers such as the reel number (ex. C-1234) or MIKAN number (ex. MIKAN 56789) in the search bar to narrow the scope of your results.
            </li>
            <li>
              When you find relevant records, download them with the <code>IMG</code>, <code>PAGE</code>, and
              <code>FULL</code> options (if available), and copy the persistent URL from the record.
            </li>
            <li>
              For citations, please follow the <a href="<%= citation_guide_path(lang: I18n.locale) %>">Citation Guide</a>:
              cite Canadiana as the source and include the persistent URL. For publicly available works, please send a
              copy or link to CRKN.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="site-section about-heritage-faq">
    <div class="canadiana-shell">
      <h2>FAQ</h2>

      <div class="about-heritage-faq__list">
        <details open>
          <summary>Is full-text search available now?</summary>
          <p>Due to its handwritten nature, most of the content in Héritage is not yet available for full-text search.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>Will full-text search be available in the future?</summary>
          <p>
            Transcribing these records to produce searchable text will take time and resources and is part of our
            longer-term work on the <a href="https://www.crkn-rcdr.ca/en/future-canadiana" target="_blank" rel="noopener">future of Canadiana</a>.
            Future versions of Héritage will support full-text searching as records are transcribed.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>What comes next for research support?</summary>
          <p>
            Over the coming years, additional research tools and infrastructure for enhanced metadata creation and
            linking will be developed by CRKN.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
